<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rufinus of Aquileia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 345–411</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Tyrannius Rufinus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rufinus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Christian monk, historian and translator who lived for many years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. His Ecclesiastical History, a translation and continuation of Eusebius completed around 402, contains the earliest account of the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> through a captive Christian woman, which he said he heard from the Iberian prince Bacurius. He is also known for his translations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/origen">Origen</a> and his quarrel with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rufinus-of-aquileia.jpg" alt="Rufinus of Aquileia" width="320" height="522" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bloemaert, Frederick (Bloemaert, Frederick) From: 1614— To: 1669 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rufinus_Aquileiensis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
